{
  "id": "37118s9",
  "title": "CHAPTER 2: MOTION",
  "order": 95,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "lg1zgn6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Rest and motion are relative terms depending on the chosen frame of reference.",
        "Distance is the total path length traversed (scalar), whereas displacement is the shortest distance between initial and final positions (vector).",
        "Speed is the rate of change of distance, while velocity is the rate of change of displacement in a specified direction.",
        "Acceleration represents the rate of change of velocity over time.",
        "Equations of motion describe the mathematical relationship between initial velocity, final velocity, acceleration, time, and displacement under uniform acceleration."
      ],
      "id": "ggncviq"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Equations of Motion",
          "value": "3",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Speed",
          "value": "m/s",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Acceleration",
          "value": "m/s²",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Conversion Factor (km/h to m/s)",
          "value": "5/18",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "3fdem73"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "naz46ry",
      "content": "Motion is said to occur when an object changes its position with respect to time and a reference point.If an object does not change its position with time, it is said to be at rest."
    },
    {
      "id": "y48ubnj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "REST AND MOTION"
    },
    {
      "id": "vqfe9f0",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Rest: When an object does not change its position with time.</li><li>Motion: When an object changes its position with time.</li></ul>",
        "Rest: When an object does not change its position with time.",
        "Motion: When an object changes its position with time."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oc2s5dp",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "7uvazcv",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_7uvazcv.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "kqb19oc",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "hn0jyfb",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_hn0jyfb.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o7fmck9",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gvwe85d",
      "content": "A person sitting in a bus is at rest with respect to the bus but in motion with respect to the road."
    },
    {
      "id": "j606vev",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "TYPES OF MOTION"
    },
    {
      "id": "cdh7cgw",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "szlr0v1",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_szlr0v1.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "bxjjeiq",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "Rectilinear Motion – Motion in a straight line",
        "Circular Motion – Motion in a circular path",
        "Rotational Motion – Motion about an axis",
        "Oscillatory Motion – To and fro motion"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xmbg72o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "DISTANCE AND DISPLACEMENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "300n11n",
      "content": "Distance:"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7h9smr",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Total path covered by an object</li><li>Scalar quantity</li><li>Always positive</li></ul>",
        "Total path covered by an object",
        "Scalar quantity",
        "Always positive"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hjtbo0k",
      "content": "Displacement:"
    },
    {
      "id": "e5irqtr",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Shortest distance between initial and final position</li><li>Vector quantity</li><li>Can be zero</li></ul>",
        "Shortest distance between initial and final position",
        "Vector quantity",
        "Can be zero"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "hhlfz52",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SPEED AND VELOCITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uuub3o4",
      "content": "Speed:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ncs0zo1",
      "content": "Speed = Distance / Time Unit = m/s"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bxcv8ik",
      "content": "Scalar quantity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sonbhtr",
      "content": "Velocity:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7wcrdnw",
      "content": "Velocity = Displacement / Time Vector quantity"
    },
    {
      "id": "bosx0xb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ACCELERATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4ejm1ga",
      "content": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o27ojs2",
      "content": "Acceleration = (Final velocity – Initial velocity) / Time Unit = m/s²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wipl4jb",
      "content": "Negative acceleration is called retardation."
    },
    {
      "id": "qkrtj2t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EQUATIONS OF MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i0kkt21",
      "content": "There are three equations of motion:"
    },
    {
      "id": "cw7kfh8",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "v = u + at",
        "s = ut + ½at²",
        "v² = u² + 2as"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9kd8aoa",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u1pr4r1",
      "content": "u = initial velocity v = final velocity a = acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Distance",
        "Displacement"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nature",
          "Scalar quantity (Magnitude only)",
          "Vector quantity (Magnitude and Direction)"
        ],
        [
          "Path Dependency",
          "Depends on the actual path covered",
          "Independent of path; depends only on initial and final points"
        ],
        [
          "Value",
          "Always positive or zero",
          "Can be positive, negative, or zero"
        ],
        [
          "Magnitude Relation",
          "Distance ≥ |Displacement|",
          "|Displacement| ≤ Distance"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "b910zwt"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "rcg9v7o",
      "content": "t = time"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "y3cdmf4",
      "content": "s = distance"
    },
    {
      "id": "3az3xur",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "UNIFORM AND NON-UNIFORM MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7qo6b1r",
      "content": "Uniform Motion:"
    },
    {
      "id": "317roo6",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Equal distance in equal time</li><li>Constant speed</li></ul>",
        "Equal distance in equal time",
        "Constant speed"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5muriyd",
      "content": "Non-uniform Motion:"
    },
    {
      "id": "qnwc7l2",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Unequal distance in equal time</li><li>Variable speed</li></ul>",
        "Unequal distance in equal time",
        "Variable speed"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "uxi3p7a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "GRAPHICAL REPRESENTATION OF MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yso4z6q",
      "content": "Distance-Time Graph: "
    },
    {
      "id": "9l3wxqd",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Straight line → Uniform motion</li><li>Curve → Non-uniform motion</li></ul>",
        "Straight line → Uniform motion",
        "Curve → Non-uniform motion"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zuo22ez",
      "content": "Velocity-Time Graph:"
    },
    {
      "id": "vbkf2pq",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Slope gives acceleration</li><li>Area gives distance</li></ul>",
        "Slope gives acceleration",
        "Area gives distance"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "rqt2rir",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "UNITS AND CONVERSIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Displacement can be zero even if distance is positive (e.g., completing a circular track).",
        "The slope of a Distance-Time graph gives Speed, while the slope of a Velocity-Time graph gives Acceleration.",
        "The area under a Velocity-Time graph represents total Displacement.",
        "To convert speed from km/h to m/s multiply by 5/18; to convert m/s to km/h multiply by 18/5."
      ],
      "id": "jch7wq1"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k5ebnfi",
      "content": "1 km/h = 5/18 m/s 1 m/s = 18/5 km/h"
    },
    {
      "id": "8ss6jht",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EXAM IMPORTANT QUESTIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
